    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Denza Launching \"God's Eye\" Driving Assistance in Europe, the Z9GT Will Get It First\nBell MV-75 Cheyenne II Black Hawk Replacement Moves Closer to First Test Flight\n21 photos\nPhoto: Bell\nBased on what American defense contractor Bell Textron and the U.S. Army have been saying over the past year or so, I was kind of expecting the first flight of the MV-75 Cheyenne II tiltrotor to take place by the end of this year. Yet, judging by what Bell said earlier this week, that’s highly unlikely.\nAfter it said it completed the first two wing structures for the first two test examples of the aircraft earlier this June, the defense contractor revealed that a number of the design’s most important components are now ready.\nThat includes the completion of the first nacelles that are meant to hold Rolls-Royce AE 1107F turboshaft engines – the same kind that is also deployed on the Lockheed Martin C-130J Super Hercules and the V-22 Osprey.\nAs soon as that happened, engineers set out to integrate the nacelles with the wings and successfully completed that task as well. At the same time, the company says it began testing the gearboxes that are meant to power several of the aircraft’s components, including the electrical power generators, hydraulic pumps, and cooling blowers for the engine and nacelle.\nOther teams are working on getting other major components and systems ready for duty, including the flight control computers, general-purpose computers, actuator drive unit, electrical components, and large area displays.\nWhen ready for duty, the MV-75 Cheyenne II will slowly but surely replace the aging UH-60 Black Hawk in the Army’s arsenal, promising to deliver twice the speed and twice the range of the iconic helicopter.\nIn numbers we can all understand, that would be a top speed of 345 mph (555 kph) and a maximum distance that can be reached on a single fill of the tanks of 575 miles (925 km). Partially responsible for that is the fact that the Cheyenne can lift off and land just like a helicopter, while traveling through the air like a conventional fixed-wing aircraft. The tiltrotor will be capable of transporting 12 soldiers and 12,000 pounds (5.4 tons) of cargo.\nThe Cheyenne is the winner of the Army’s Future Long Range Assault Aircraft (FLRAA) program, and it was first referred to as the V-280 Valor. Despite being significantly different from the helicopter it is meant to replace, it will pretty much be used in the same (and many) roles: troop insertion, medevac, long-range air assault operations, and disaster relief, among others.\nIt’s unclear how many of these tiltrotors will eventually deploy, but if we are to judge by the size of the Black Hawk fleet, we’re probably talking about thousands of them. Each Cheyenne is expected to cost the Army at least $43 million, about double the cost of the Black Hawk currently in use.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMV-75 Cheyenne II Tiltrotor Bell\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Porsche's Panamera Is Getting a Facelift Less Than 3 Years After Its Debut",
